      "id": "notes/eight-japanese-principles-for-habits",
      "type": "Note",
      "title": "Eight Japanese principles for building habits",
      "excerpt": "Eight ideas for starting and keeping habits, saved from a carousel post and rewritten in my own words. The through-line is that none of them ask for more motivation. They lower the cost of starting, or make stopping the harder option.",
      "body": "![Eight everyday objects arranged as a collection of small repeatable practices](assets/posts/eight-japanese-principles-for-habits.jpg)\n\nEight ideas for starting and keeping habits, saved from a carousel post and\nrewritten in my own words. The through-line is that none of them ask for\nmore motivation. They lower the cost of starting, or make stopping the\nharder option.\n\n**1. Kaizen: the one-minute rule.** Start with something so small the brain\ncan't object: one push-up, one sentence, one minute of the thing.\n\n**2. Ikigai: a reason to get up.** The question isn't what you do, it's why\nyou get up. A clear \"why\" makes the effort cheaper.\n\n**3. Hara hachi bu: stop at 80% full.** Eating past comfortable costs focus\nand energy. Leaving a little room keeps the afternoon usable.\n\n**4. Anchor the focus: build a ritual.** Twenty-five minutes on, five off,\nopened by the same small cue each time: a breath, a phrase, a gesture.\nRepetition teaches the brain what's about to happen.\n\n**5. Seiri and seiton: sort, then set in order.** Clutter is mental noise.\nA tidied space isn't vanity, it's fewer decisions before starting.\n\n**6. Kintsugi: finish it even when it's flawed.** Procrastination is often\nfear of a bad result. Finishing builds momentum; waiting for perfect keeps\nyou still.\n\n**7. Wabi-sabi: act before conditions are right.** Start with what you\nhave. A lot of procrastination is fear wearing patience as a disguise.\n\n**8. Build a system: track the progress.** Motivation alone doesn't change\nbehaviour. Visible progress does, and a system outlasts the mood that\nstarted it.\n\nWorth remembering that kintsugi and wabi-sabi are aesthetic philosophies\nabout repair and impermanence rather than productivity advice; this is the\npost's framing of them, not their original meaning.\n\nSource: @themillionairemagnate on Instagram, seen 1 September 2026.",
      "view": "cards",
      "topics": [
        "habits",
        "adhd"
      ],
